-- title:
--   Torsion-lift ring S represents q-torsion lifts of ̄ Q_k
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a prime $\ell$ with $3 \le \ell$, a non-zero natural number $M'$, and a commutative ring $A_0$.
--
--   **Equivariance of the level structures over $A_0$.** The hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM` assert that the two level structures used below are compatible with Weierstrass variable changes over every commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$: for every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$, (i) if a quadruple $D=(x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ is a [`ModularCurve.IsLevelPStructure`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L104) for $W$ at $\ell$ — both $(x_P,y_P)$ and $(x_Q,y_Q)$ satisfy the affine Weierstrass equation, both $x_P$ and $x_Q$ are roots of $W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi\,\ell$, and the two elements $\mathrm{indepElt}\,W\,\ell\,x_P\,x_Q$ and $\mathrm{indepElt}\,W\,\ell\,x_Q\,x_P$, namely the products $\prod_{a=1}^{(\ell-1)/2}\bigl(x\cdot(W.\Psi\mathrm{Sq}\,a)(x_0)-(W.\Phi\,a)(x_0)\bigr)$, are units — then `D.variableChange C` is such a structure for $C \bullet W$; and (ii) if $h \in T[X]$ satisfies [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) (by definition `W.IsTwoKernel h` when $p^k = 2$, and `W.IsCyclicGenKernel p k h` otherwise) then [`ModularCurve.kernelVariableChangeDeg C (ModularCurve.gamma0PowDeg p k) h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassLevelComponents.html#L104) satisfies the same condition for $C \bullet W$.
--
--   **Group laws, transport, and the moduli package over $A_0$.** Further data over $A_0$: a family $\mathcal{G}$ of relative group laws, attaching to every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every witness that $\Delta_W$ is a unit a relative group law on the Proj model `projModelStrCR W` over $\operatorname{Spec} T$; the hypothesis `h𝒢` that $\mathcal{G}$ is chord–tangent (for each such $W$ there is a family of bijections between the $F$-valued points of the Proj model and the affine points of the base change $W_F$, for $F$ a field over $T$, which is additive and compatible with Galois twisting); the hypothesis `h𝒢O` that $\mathcal{G}$ has the origin as identity (for each such $W$ the identity section is presented by a ring homomorphism from the origin chart ring of $W$ killing $x/y$ and $z/y$); a level transport $\mathcal{T}$, which transports raw Drinfeld pairs (a projective curve together with two sections $P,Q$) functorially along $A_0$-algebra maps and along variable changes, preserving the condition `RawDrinfeldPair.IsLevel 𝒢 q` (the curve is the prescribed one and, for some witness of unit discriminant, $(P,Q)$ is a Drinfeld basis of level $q$ for the associated group law, i.e. the basis divisor equals the $q$-torsion ideal); and the hypothesis `h𝒯` that $\mathcal{T}$ is a section transport, in the two clauses (variable changes, algebra maps) which identify the transported sections with the pullbacks of $P$ and $Q$ along the induced morphisms of Proj models.
--
--   Finally $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for the moduli datum of `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, i.e. for the functor sending an $A_0$-algebra $T$ to the set of variable-change classes of tuples consisting of a Weierstrass curve over $T$ with unit discriminant, a family of polynomials indexed by the prime factors $p$ of $M'$ satisfying `IsGamma0PowAt` at $(p, M'.\mathrm{factorization}\,p)$, a level-$\ell$ structure, and a raw Drinfeld pair of level $q$; thus $P_0$ consists of a ring `P₀.B₀` over $A_0$, a point `P₀.univ` over it, and the universal property that every point over every $T$ is the image of `P₀.univ` under a unique $A_0$-algebra map.
--
--   **The local rings and the residue field.** $R$ is a complete Noetherian local ring (complete for its maximal-ideal adic topology) which is an $A_0$-algebra, with an $A_0$-algebra map $\iota : P_0.B_0 \to R$; $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which the images of $\ell$ and of $M'$ are non-zero (`hℓk`, `hM'k`), together with a surjective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal of $R$; $W_0$ is a complete discrete valuation ring which is a domain, with maximal ideal generated by $q$ (`hW₀`) and a surjective $\mathrm{res}_0 : W_0 \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal; $R$ is a $W_0$-algebra, $W_0$ an $A_0$-algebra, the tower $A_0 \to W_0 \to R$ is compatible, and $\mathrm{res}_R$ restricts to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$ (`hresR₀`). The hypothesis `hfac` is the factorisation property of the pair $(R,\iota)$: for every Artinian local ring $T$ which is a $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra with compatible tower, every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$, and every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : P_0.B_0 \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   **The étale package over $W_0$.** The hypotheses `hℓ'` and `hM'` are the analogues of `hℓ` and `hM` over $W_0$-algebras, and `h𝒢r`, `h𝒢Or` state that the restriction of scalars of $\mathcal{G}$ to $W_0$ is chord–tangent and has the origin as identity. Then $P^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` over $W_0$ for the moduli datum of the rigid data attached to the product of `gamma0PowComponent W₀ M' hM'`, `levelPComponent W₀ ℓ hℓ'` and the trivial component (whose values are a one-point type and whose level condition is vacuous); $x^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a raw representative over `Pet.B₀` — a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant, a family of $\Gamma_0$-generator polynomials, a level-$\ell$ datum and the trivial datum, with the corresponding level conditions — whose class in the quotient is `Pet.univ` (`hxet`).
--
--   **The ring representing $q$-torsion sections.** $CQ$ is a commutative ring which is a `Pet.B₀`-algebra and a $W_0$-algebra with compatible tower, and $Q_u$ is a section of the Proj model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to CQ$. The hypothesis `hQu` states that the $q$-fold sum of $Q_u$ for the group law $(\mathcal{G}$ restricted to $W_0)$ on the Proj model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ is the identity section, and `hrep` states that $CQ$ represents $q$-torsion: for every `Pet.B₀`-algebra $T$ and every $T$-point $Q$ of that Proj model, the $q$-fold sum of $Q$ is the identity section if and only if there is a unique `Pet.B₀`-algebra map $\chi : CQ \to T$ such that $\operatorname{Spec}\chi$ followed by $Q_u$ equals $Q$.
--
--   **The torsion-lift stalk $S$ and its formal group.** $S$ is a complete Noetherian local $W_0$-algebra with a $W_0$-algebra map $\iota_S : CQ \to S$, a surjection $\mathrm{res}_S : S \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal, restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$, and `hfacS` is the corresponding factorisation property of $(S,\iota_S)$: for every Artinian local $W_0$-algebra $T$ with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T$ onto $k$ with kernel the maximal ideal and restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$, and every $W_0$-algebra map $\varphi : CQ \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : S \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_S$ and $\Phi \circ \iota_S = \varphi$. Moreover $F_S$ is a formal group over $S$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base changed along $\mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to CQ \xrightarrow{\iota_S} S$ (`hFS`), and the $q$-series $F_S.\mathrm{nthSeries}\,q$ (the iterated addition series) factors as $X \cdot g \cdot v$ (`hfacq`), where $g \in S[X]$ is monic of degree $q-1$ with all coefficients of index $< q-1$ in the maximal ideal of $S$ and constant coefficient of the form $q\,w$ with $w$ a unit, and $v$ is a unit power series.
--
--   **The $k$-point being lifted.** The field $k$ is an $A_0$-, $W_0$- and `Pet.B₀`-algebra, with compatible tower $A_0 \to W_0 \to k$, and the structure map of $W_0$ is $\mathrm{res}_0$ (`hk₀`). There are an $A_0$-algebra map $\rho : P_0.B_0 \to k$ equal to $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$ (`hρ`) and a $W_0$-algebra map $\psi^{\mathrm{et}} : \mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to k$ equal to $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$ composed with $\mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to CQ$ (`hψet`), and $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ is the algebra structure map of $k$ over `Pet.B₀` (`hψalg`). The discriminant of the reduced curve $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base changed along $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a unit (`hΔk`), and $Q_k$ is a $k$-section of the Proj model of that reduced curve. The hypothesis `hQk_pin` pins $Q_k$ as the reduction of $Q_u$: there are a graded ring homomorphism $\phi$ from the graded ring of the Proj model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ to that of its reduction, and a proof that the irrelevant ideal of the target lies in the image under $\phi$ of the irrelevant ideal of the source, such that $\phi$ is a coefficient homomorphism for $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ (it sends the class of a constant $a$ to the class of $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}(a)$ and fixes the classes of the three coordinates) and $Q_k$ followed by $\mathrm{Proj.map}\,\phi$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$. The hypothesis `hQk` states that $Q_k$ is not the identity section of the group law $\mathcal{G}$ on the reduced curve.
--
--   The remaining hypotheses describe the reduced tuple: `hhk` states that for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$ the polynomial $(x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.1\,p)$ reduced along $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ satisfies `IsGamma0PowAt` for the reduced curve at $(p, M'.\mathrm{factorization}\,p)$; `hDk` states that the reduced level-$\ell$ datum is a level-$\ell$ structure on the reduced curve; `hyk` states that the raw Drinfeld pair consisting of the reduced curve, the identity section, and $Q_k$ is of level $q$ for $\mathcal{G}$; and `hρyk` states that the image of `P₀.univ` under $\rho$ is the class of the raw datum over $k$ assembled from the reduced curve, `hΔk`, the reduced $\Gamma_0$-polynomials, the reduced level-$\ell$ datum and that Drinfeld pair, together with the proofs `hhk`, `hDk`, `hyk`.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses there exists a family $\beta$ which assigns to every Artinian local ring $T$ that is a $W_0$-algebra, and to every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ and restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$, a bijection
--   $$\Bigl\{\, d = (d_1, d_2) \ \Bigm|\ d_1 : \mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to_{W_0} T,\ d_2 : \operatorname{Spec} T \longrightarrow \mathrm{projModelCR}\,(x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}) \,\Bigr\} \;\simeq\; (S \to_{W_0} T),$$
--   where the left-hand side consists of those pairs subject to three conditions: first, $\mathrm{res}_T \circ d_1 = \psi^{\mathrm{et}}$; second, there is a proof $h_Q$ that $d_2$ followed by `projModelStrCR` of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ equals $\operatorname{Spec} d_1$ — so that $d_2$ is a section over $\operatorname{Spec} d_1$ — and the $q$-fold sum of the section $\langle d_2, h_Q\rangle$ for the group law $(\mathcal{G}$ restricted to $W_0)$ on the Proj model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ is the identity section over that base morphism; third, $\operatorname{Spec}\mathrm{res}_T$ followed by $d_2$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$.
--
--   The bijections are given together with an explicit description of their inverses: for every such $T$, $\mathrm{res}_T$ and accompanying proofs, and every $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : S \to T$, the pair underlying $\beta^{-1}(\Phi)$ is
--   $$\bigl(\ (\Phi \circ \iota_S) \circ (\mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to CQ),\ \ \operatorname{Spec}(\Phi \circ \iota_S) \ \text{followed by}\ Q_u \ \bigr),$$
--   that is, the first component is the composite of the structure map $\mathrm{Pet.B}_0 \to CQ$ with $\Phi \circ \iota_S$, and the second is the pullback of $Q_u$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\Phi \circ \iota_S$.
--
--   This is the representability statement for the functor of $q$-torsion lifts on the ordinary (étale) locus, in the style of the Igusa-tower computations of Katz–Mazur: the complete local ring $S$, built over the ring $CQ$ that represents $q$-torsion sections of the universal étale curve, carries the universal lift of the non-trivial $q$-torsion point $Q_k$ over the residue field $k$, and the bijection is inverse to pullback of the universal section $Q_u$. It is the input to the next step, where the $q$-torsion lifts over Artinian test rings are matched with the roots of the monic degree $q-1$ factor $g$ of the $q$-series of the formal group.
